1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,960
Can you show me Pods?

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:10,877
A Pod is the smallest thing Kubernetes runs:

3
00:00:10,877 --> 00:00:14,561
one or more containers that always run together,

4
00:00:14,561 --> 00:00:20,010
on the same node, sharing one network address and, optionally, storage.

5
00:00:20,010 --> 00:00:26,610
Kubernetes never runs a container on its own; it runs Pods, and a Pod runs containers.

6
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:27,224
Analogy:

7
00:00:27,224 --> 00:00:31,215
a container is a person; a Pod is a small apartment.

8
00:00:31,215 --> 00:00:33,825
Most apartments have one resident.

9
00:00:34,367 --> 00:00:36,550
Some processes belong together:

10
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:43,593
a web server and a helper that refreshes its files, an application and an agent that ships its logs.

11
00:00:43,593 --> 00:00:48,594
They must run on the same machine, talk over localhost and share files.

12
00:00:48,594 --> 00:00:51,481
The Pod is the unit that guarantees this.

13
00:00:53,233 --> 00:00:55,653
And how does it actually work?

14
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:56,734
Pod IP:

15
00:00:56,734 --> 00:01:04,213
every Pod gets its own IP address inside the cluster; all its containers share it (and its ports).

16
00:01:04,213 --> 00:01:06,121
Shared network namespace:

17
00:01:06,121 --> 00:01:14,744
containers in a Pod reach each other on localhost; two containers in one Pod cannot both listen on the same port.

18
00:01:14,744 --> 00:01:15,889
Shared volumes:

19
00:01:15,889 --> 00:01:21,612
a volume declared in the Pod can be mounted into several of its containers.

20
00:01:21,612 --> 00:01:22,986
Lifecycle (phase):

21
00:01:22,986 --> 00:01:25,657
Pending (accepted, not running yet:

22
00:01:25,657 --> 00:01:35,120
scheduling, image download) → Running (at least one container running) → Succeeded (all containers exited with 0) or Failed.

23
00:01:36,867 --> 00:01:39,291
A namespace for this lesson:

24
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:39,990
1.

25
00:01:39,990 --> 00:01:43,128
Create the first Pod and watch it start:

26
00:01:43,667 --> 00:01:43,836
2.

27
00:01:43,836 --> 00:01:46,381
Describe it and read its logs:

28
00:01:46,933 --> 00:01:47,103
2.

29
00:01:47,103 --> 00:01:49,648
Describe it and read its logs:

30
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:54,309
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

31
00:01:54,867 --> 00:02:03,151
With more than one container, logs and exec need -c NAME; without it kubectl picks the first container and says so.

32
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:06,430
Let me try something.

33
00:02:06,430 --> 00:02:11,020
A Pod whose container exits with an error right after starting:

34
00:02:11,567 --> 00:02:13,135
What should happen?

35
00:02:13,135 --> 00:02:14,787
A running container.

36
00:02:14,787 --> 00:02:15,943
What happened?

37
00:02:15,943 --> 00:02:19,741
CrashLoopBackOff and a growing RESTARTS count:

38
00:02:19,741 --> 00:02:23,209
the container starts, exits, is restarted,

39
00:02:23,209 --> 00:02:28,906
exits again, and the kubelet waits longer each time (the "back-off").

40
00:02:30,667 --> 00:02:33,576
A Pod's containers cannot be changed in place:

41
00:02:33,576 --> 00:02:37,561
delete the Pod and create it with a command that keeps running.

42
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:40,013
Got it.

43
00:02:40,013 --> 00:02:42,764
What do I need to remember?

44
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:50,459
A Pod is one or more containers on one node, sharing an IP (localhost) and volumes.

45
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:51,576
Phases:

46
00:02:51,576 --> 00:02:58,564
Pending → Running → Succeeded/Failed; CrashLoopBackOff = the container keeps exiting.

47
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:04,669
describe (events, exit code) and logs --previous explain crashes.

48
00:03:06,433 --> 00:03:11,197
I keep hearing about labels, selectors and annotations.

49
00:03:11,197 --> 00:03:12,843
What is it, really?

50
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:16,533
A label is a key/value tag on an object:

51
00:03:16,533 --> 00:03:16,847
app:

52
00:03:16,847 --> 00:03:18,178
web, environment:

53
00:03:18,178 --> 00:03:18,962
dev, tier:

54
00:03:18,962 --> 00:03:19,667
frontend.

55
00:03:19,667 --> 00:03:22,644
A selector is a question about labels:

56
00:03:22,644 --> 00:03:26,404
"all objects with app=web and environment=prod".

57
00:03:26,404 --> 00:03:33,219
An annotation is also a key/value pair, but for information meant for people and tools,

58
00:03:33,219 --> 00:03:38,154
never for selecting (a description, a contact, a build number).

59
00:03:38,154 --> 00:03:38,781
Analogy:

60
00:03:38,781 --> 00:03:43,481
labels are the coloured stickers on moving boxes ("kitchen",

61
00:03:43,481 --> 00:03:48,964
"fragile"); a selector is "bring me every box with a kitchen sticker".

62
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:54,802
This is the most important idea for understanding how Kubernetes connects things.

63
00:03:54,802 --> 00:03:58,337
Kubernetes objects do not point at each other by name:

64
00:03:58,337 --> 00:04:03,835
a ReplicaSet does not keep a list of "its" Pods, a Service does not list "its" Pods.

65
00:04:03,835 --> 00:04:09,399
They hold a selector, and whatever currently carries matching labels belongs to them.

66
00:04:11,133 --> 00:04:13,553
And how does it actually work?

67
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:15,562
Equality-based:

68
00:04:15,562 --> 00:04:18,194
app=web, environment!=prod.

69
00:04:18,194 --> 00:04:19,169
Set-based:

70
00:04:19,169 --> 00:04:27,260
environment in (dev,test), tier notin (backend), app (has the key), !app (has not).

71
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:33,109
Several conditions separated by commas must all match (AND).

72
00:04:33,109 --> 00:04:41,785
Keys may have a prefix (app.kubernetes.io/name); values are short strings (63 characters,

73
00:04:41,785 --> 00:04:44,319
letters, digits, -, _, .).

74
00:04:46,067 --> 00:04:46,301
1.

75
00:04:46,301 --> 00:04:48,061
See the labels:

76
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:48,769
1.

77
00:04:48,769 --> 00:04:50,036
See the labels:

78
00:04:50,036 --> 00:04:53,245
-L key shows chosen labels as columns:

79
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:56,949
-L key shows chosen labels as columns:

80
00:04:57,500 --> 00:04:57,689
2.

81
00:04:57,689 --> 00:04:59,677
Select with equality:

82
00:04:59,677 --> 00:04:59,866
3.

83
00:04:59,866 --> 00:05:01,474
Select with sets:

84
00:05:03,233 --> 00:05:06,143
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

85
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:13,024
The same selectors work for every kind and for deletion, which makes them powerful and dangerous:

86
00:05:14,767 --> 00:05:16,368
Let me try something.

87
00:05:16,368 --> 00:05:20,411
Look for the web Pods with a small typo in the value:

88
00:05:20,967 --> 00:05:22,600
What should happen?

89
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:23,718
Two web Pods.

90
00:05:23,718 --> 00:05:24,922
What happened?

91
00:05:24,922 --> 00:05:27,846
No resources found, with no error:

92
00:05:27,846 --> 00:05:33,951
a selector that matches nothing is not an error, it is an empty answer.

93
00:05:35,700 --> 00:05:37,499
Here is the fix.

94
00:05:39,233 --> 00:05:39,947
Got it.

95
00:05:39,947 --> 00:05:42,698
What do I need to remember?

96
00:05:43,233 --> 00:05:51,298
Labels tag objects; selectors find objects by labels; annotations hold information that is never selected.

97
00:05:51,833 --> 00:05:59,678
Services, ReplicaSets, Deployments and NetworkPolicies find their Pods with selectors, continuously.

98
00:06:00,233 --> 00:06:02,502
An empty selection is not an error:

99
00:06:02,502 --> 00:06:06,068
check labels with --show-labels when "nothing happens".
